# Young adults (part c): facilitator scripts, tasks and witness stories.
R = {
'YA-6-1': dict(
  saint="Adam and Eve: the first man and woman, who greeted each other with a cry of joy.",
  tryit=["Read Genesis 2:18-25 slowly.", "Write what the man’s cry tells us about love.", "Write one sentence on what the body says."],
  saintStory="The second chapter of Genesis tells how God formed the man from the dust of the ground and put him in the garden. The man named the animals, but “for the man there was not found a helper fit for him.” God said, “It is not good that the man should be alone.” So God caused a deep sleep to fall upon him, and from his side he formed a woman, and brought her to the man.\nThe man’s response is the first recorded human speech in the Bible, and it is a poem: “This at last is bone of my bones and flesh of my flesh; she shall be called Woman, because she was taken out of Man.” It is a cry of wonder and recognition. The text goes on: “Therefore a man leaves his father and his mother and cleaves to his wife, and they become one flesh. And the man and his wife were both naked, and were not ashamed.”\nBefore the fall, there was no shame. The two looked at each other without fear of being used. John Paul II, in his Theology of the Body, reflected on this “original nakedness” as showing what love was meant to be: complete self-gift without exploitation. Adam and Eve are the first picture of what we are made for.",
  script=dict(
    open="Say: “Everyone wants to be loved. What do you think love is? Give me one sentence, honest or cynical.” Take answers around the room. Write a few on the board. Then say, “Let’s see what the Church says that love is, and what the body has to do with it.”",
    teach=["Say: “Genesis 1:27: ‘So God created man in his own image, in the image of God he created him; male and female he created them.’ The Church teaches that sexual difference is a good gift of God, and that both men and women have equal dignity as persons. We are made for communion: ‘It is not good that the man should be alone.’ The Catechism: ‘God is love and in himself he lives a mystery of personal loving communion. Creating the human race in his own image… God inscribed in the humanity of man and woman the vocation, and thus the capacity and responsibility, of love and communion.’ (CCC 2331) Love is our vocation. The body is not a shell but a part of the person. It expresses who we are. John Paul II’s Theology of the Body (talks 1979-84): the body reveals the person, and the ‘nuptial meaning of the body’ is the capacity to express love by self-gift.”", "Say: “What is love? Aquinas: to love is to will the good of the other. John Paul II in Love and Responsibility: the opposite of love is not hate but use, treating a person as an object for my pleasure. Love is the affirmation of the person for their own sake. The Greek words: eros (desire), philia (friendship), agape (self-giving love). Benedict XVI’s Deus Caritas Est (2005) says that eros and agape belong together: eros must be purified and healed by agape. Love involves emotion, but isn’t reducible to it; it includes commitment. Sexuality is a profound language of the body: it speaks total self-gift, faithful, free, fruitful. The Church’s teaching on chastity and marriage comes from this. Sex outside marriage ‘lies’ with the body: it says ‘I give you everything’ without the commitment that makes it true.”", "Say: “Why does the Church say sexual intimacy belongs in marriage? Because it is both unitive (it joins) and procreative (it is open to life). Marriage is a lifelong covenant that gives the safe space for total self-giving. This is demanding. Our culture says otherwise: sex as recreation, hooking up, ‘as long as it’s consensual.’ The Church doesn’t deny desire or pleasure; she insists they are goods that need a worthy context. The result of ignoring this: wounds—loneliness, regret, the sense of being used. If you’ve experienced that, mercy is available; you don’t have to be defined by your past. Chastity is for everyone: not repression but integration and freedom (CCC 2337). We’re called to love well in every state of life: single, dating, married, religious. Let’s explore what that means.”"],
    ask=["What does it mean that we are made in God’s image as male and female? (We are made for loving communion; both sexes share equal dignity.)", "What did John Paul II say is the opposite of love? (Use, treating a person as an object.)", "Why does the Church link sexual intimacy with marriage? (It expresses total, faithful, fruitful self-gift.)"],
    practice=["Read Genesis 2:18-25 aloud. Underline the words that express wonder, relationship and trust.", "In pairs, discuss: How does our culture’s image of love differ from this? What does it get right? What does it miss?", "Write your own definition of love in a sentence, using the ideas of gift and commitment.", "Identify one relationship in your life where you can practice self-gift this week."],
    close="Pray Psalm 139:13-14 together: “I praise you, for I am fearfully and wonderfully made.” Sign of the Cross. Suggest the group read the first three talks of Theology of the Body (or a summary) before next time.")),

'YA-6-2': dict(
  saint="Isaac and Rebekah: a courtship that began with prayer and ended with her free answer, “I will go.”",
  tryit=["List what you want in a marriage partner.", "Separate what is essential from what is preference.", "Set one boundary you will keep."],
  saintStory="In Genesis 24, Abraham, old and wanting to see his son Isaac married, sends his chief servant to his homeland to find a wife for Isaac among his relatives. The servant takes ten camels, and arrives at a well outside the city at evening, when the women come out to draw water. He prays: “O Lord, God of my master Abraham, grant me success today… Let the young woman to whom I shall say, ‘Pray let down your jar that I may drink,’ and who shall say, ‘Drink, and I will water your camels’—let her be the one whom you have appointed for your servant Isaac.”\nBefore he finished praying, Rebekah came out with her jar. She gave him water and said, “I will draw for your camels also.” She watered all ten camels, an enormous task. Her family learned the servant’s purpose and agreed. But they asked her, “Will you go with this man?” She said, “I will go.”\nOn the journey home, she saw a man walking in the field toward evening: Isaac, who had gone out to meditate. She veiled herself. Isaac took her into his mother’s tent, “and she became his wife; and he loved her. So Isaac was comforted after his mother’s death.” The story shows prayer, character, family involvement, her free consent, and love.",
  script=dict(
    open="Ask: “What is the hardest part of dating or discerning a relationship today? What do people get wrong? What do people do well?” Allow honest answers. Say, “The Church does not give a dating manual, but she gives principles that protect love.”",
    teach=["Say: “Dating is the time of getting to know someone in order to discern whether marriage is God’s call. Its purpose is discernment, not just fun or companionship (though these are good). Questions to ask: Do we share faith and values? Does this person bring me closer to God? Do we respect each other? Can we communicate honestly? Does this person show good character, such as honesty, generosity, kindness, responsibility? How do they treat others? Are there warning signs: controlling behavior, disrespect, abuse, addiction? In Genesis 24, character was the test: Rebekah’s generosity. Also notice the roles of prayer, family, and her free consent. Consent is essential to a valid marriage (CCC 1626).”", "Say: “Boundaries. Chastity in dating means respecting each other as persons, avoiding actions that treat the other as an object or that pull toward sex outside marriage. The Church teaches that sexual relations are reserved for marriage. That includes sexual acts short of intercourse that are directed to arousal. Practical boundaries: decide in advance; avoid situations of isolation and temptation; limit physical affection; dress modestly; keep conversations honest; pray together; have friends and community around; set time limits at night; avoid pressure; talk openly about expectations. Cohabitation: the Church teaches it is wrong and also statistically linked with higher risk of break-up. If you have already crossed lines, mercy is available: Confession, a fresh start, and decisions about boundaries going forward.”", "Say: “Discerning marriage. Consider: Are we free to marry? Do we agree about children, faith, finances, family? Have we done an engagement and a marriage preparation course? Have we spoken to a priest? Do our families know and support us? Are we ready to promise for life? Break-ups are painful, but if the relationship isn’t leading to marriage, better to end it honestly, kindly. The Catechism notes that engagement (the betrothal) is a time of preparation for marriage (CCC 1632-1633): couples are called to chastity. Singleness is a valid and holy state, not a failure. Don’t settle; don’t idolize marriage. Pray for your future spouse. Pray that God will show you whether he wants you for marriage or another vocation. Like Isaac, we can wait, be quiet, and meditate.”"],
    ask=["What is the purpose of dating? (Discernment of whether marriage is God’s call.)", "What does chastity mean in dating? (Respecting each other as persons, keeping sexual intimacy for marriage.)", "What is essential for a valid marriage? (Free consent of both.)"],
    practice=["Make two lists: what I want in a spouse; what I bring. Mark what is essential (faith, character, commitment) and what is preference (looks, hobbies).", "Write three boundaries that would protect a relationship, and one person who could hold you accountable.", "In pairs, talk through: What would I do if I realized I had crossed a line? Who would I tell?", "Write a short prayer for your future vocation, whatever it is."],
    close="Pray the prayer of Tobias and Sarah (Tobit 8:5-7) or the Our Father. Sign of the Cross. Encourage everyone to read Genesis 24 at home.")),

'YA-6-3': dict(
  saint="Margaret of Cortona: a young woman who lived for nine years as a nobleman’s mistress, then spent thirty years in penance and service.",
  tryit=["Write a plan for freedom in four parts.", "Name one person you will tell.", "Name one change in your phone or devices."],
  saintStory="Margaret was born in 1247 in Tuscany, Italy, into a farming family. Her mother died when she was seven. Her stepmother was cold to her. At seventeen, she ran off with a young nobleman from Montepulciano, who promised marriage but never married her. For about nine years, she lived in his castle as his mistress and bore him a son. She enjoyed luxury, fine clothes and the attention of the town.\nIn 1273, her lover went out and did not return. His dog came back and led her to the place where his body lay, murdered. Margaret was shaken. She realized the emptiness of her life. She left the castle with her son and went back to her father’s house, but he refused to receive her, because of his wife. She was alone and poor with a child.\nShe turned to the Franciscans in Cortona, who accepted her. She spent years in penance, and in 1277 became a Third Order Franciscan. She cared for the sick and poor, begged for them, and founded a hospital. She was known for her hours of prayer and her gentleness. She died in 1297 and was canonized in 1728. Her life shows that no past is beyond mercy.",
  script=dict(
    open="Say plainly, with warmth: “This is a topic where shame can silence people. In this room, nobody has to share anything about themselves. Our aim is freedom and mercy, not blame.” Ask: “What do you think pornography teaches about people? About ourselves?” Take a few general answers.",
    teach=["Say: “Pornography is widespread, and most young adults have encountered it. The Church speaks clearly: ‘Pornography consists in removing real or simulated sexual acts from the intimacy of the partners, in order to display them deliberately to third parties. It offends against chastity because it perverts the conjugal act… It does grave injury to the dignity of its participants (actors, vendors, the public), since each one becomes an object of base pleasure and illicit profit for others.’ (CCC 2354) It is gravely wrong because it uses persons as objects, and because of how it affects viewers: it distorts desire, can become compulsive (neuroscience research shows addictive patterns), damages relationships and the ability to love. It often is linked to exploitation and trafficking.”", "Say: “But the Church speaks mercy even more. ‘There is therefore now no condemnation for those who are in Christ Jesus.’ (Romans 8:1) ‘If the Son makes you free, you will be free indeed.’ (John 8:36) ‘God is faithful, and he will not let you be tempted beyond your strength, but with the temptation will also provide the way of escape.’ (1 Corinthians 10:13) Shame says, ‘You are disgusting; hide.’ The Gospel says, ‘You are loved; come into the light.’ Compare the woman caught in adultery: Jesus did not condemn her, and said, ‘Go, and do not sin again.’ (John 8:11) Both parts are essential. The Church recognizes that compulsion can lessen culpability, but it still harms and needs healing.”", "Say: “Practical help. Grace: frequent Confession, the Eucharist, prayer, the Rosary, Scripture. Honesty: tell a trusted person: a friend, a priest, a counselor. Secrets feed addiction. Structure: filters and accountability software on phones and computers; keep devices out of the bedroom; avoid screens late at night; delete accounts or apps that trigger you. Replace: exercise, friendships, hobbies, service. Understand triggers: stress, loneliness, boredom, tiredness (HALT: hungry, angry, lonely, tired). Professional help: therapists who treat compulsive behavior; groups like Celebrate Recovery, Sexaholics Anonymous, and Catholic ministries like Integrity Restored. Progress is not linear; relapse isn’t the end. Getting up again is the fight. For those who aren’t struggling: don’t judge; help friends; avoid supporting the industry. For those who have been harmed by someone else’s use, healing is also available.”"],
    ask=["How does the Catechism describe pornography? (It offends chastity, uses persons as objects, and gravely injures dignity.)", "What is the Gospel response to those who struggle? (No condemnation; freedom in Christ; grace and community.)", "Name two practical steps. (Tell a trusted person; use filters; Confession; professional help.)"],
    practice=["Write a four-part plan for freedom: one step for devices, one for community (a person to tell), one for prayer and sacraments, one for body and sleep.", "Share only what you choose with a partner, or write for yourself.", "If it is not your struggle, write how you can support a friend without shame.", "Pray together Psalm 51:10-12 and the Our Father."],
    close="Close with the prayer: “Lord, set me free and make my heart clean.” Sign of the Cross. Offer information about confession times and recovery resources, privately.")),

'YA-7-1': dict(
  saint="Nathanael: the skeptic who was invited with the words “Come and see.”",
  tryit=["Write your own “Come and see.”", "List the pulls and fears about the call.", "Choose one step to test it."],
  saintStory="In John’s Gospel, Philip finds Nathanael and tells him: “We have found him of whom Moses in the law and also the prophets wrote, Jesus of Nazareth, the son of Joseph.” Nathanael’s response: “Can anything good come out of Nazareth?” It is the cry of a skeptic, maybe even a snob. Philip doesn’t argue. He says, “Come and see.”\nNathanael goes. As he approaches, Jesus says, “Behold, an Israelite indeed, in whom is no guile!” Nathanael asks, “How do you know me?” Jesus answers, “Before Philip called you, when you were under the fig tree, I saw you.” Nathanael, overwhelmed, says, “Rabbi, you are the Son of God! You are the King of Israel!” Jesus says, “You shall see greater things than these… you will see heaven opened, and the angels of God ascending and descending upon the Son of man.”\nNathanael is usually identified with Bartholomew, one of the Twelve. Tradition says he carried the Gospel to India and Armenia. The story shows that the call often comes through a friend, a question, a being known. It begins with “Come and see.”",
  script=dict(
    open="Hand out paper and ask, “Write the question about your future that wakes you up at night.” After two minutes, ask people to fold the papers and exchange them. Read a few anonymously to normalize the questions.",
    teach=["Say: “The Catechism: ‘Vocation’ comes from the Latin vocare, to call. God calls every person. First, to exist and to love: ‘Before I formed you in the womb I knew you.’ (Jeremiah 1:5) Second, to holiness through Baptism: every baptized person is called to ‘the fullness of Christian life and the perfection of charity’ (LG 40). Third, to a particular state of life: marriage, ordained ministry, consecrated life or single life for the Kingdom. Fourth, to particular tasks and missions in the world, work, service. The universal call to holiness was Vatican II’s emphasis: holiness isn’t only for priests and nuns.”", "Say: “How does God call? Often through ordinary things: desires, gifts, circumstances, friends, needs, moments of grace, Scripture, prayer. Sometimes dramatically (Paul), usually quietly (Samuel). Ignatius of Loyola taught discernment of spirits: notice consolation (peace, joy, a sense of rightness, leading to God) and desolation (restlessness, anxiety, pulling away). Pay attention to what gives deep, lasting life over time. Discernment is a practice: pray, reflect, talk with a wise guide, test with small steps, and decide with freedom. God’s will is not a hidden riddle; it is often found in the next faithful step. The best question: ‘What is God inviting me to now?’ Discernment includes both the heart’s desires and the needs of the world.”", "Say: “Obstacles. Fear: ‘What if I say yes and I am unhappy?’ God’s call is for our flourishing. The fear of missing the ‘one right answer’: God can work with many good choices. Noise: constant distraction makes it hard to listen. Pressure: from family or culture. Comparison. Postponing: ‘when life settles down.’ Unwillingness: afraid of what God might ask. Helpful practices: daily prayer and silence; Eucharist and Confession; a spiritual director; a retreat; honest conversations with friends; service. Pray the Suscipe of Ignatius: ‘Take, Lord, receive all my liberty…’ Nathanael’s story: he came with doubt and found he had been seen. You are known. Come and see.”"],
    ask=["What does ‘vocation’ mean? (A call from God.)", "What are consolation and desolation? (Ignatian terms for interior movements toward or away from God.)", "What is the most important question in discernment? (What is God inviting me to now?)"],
    practice=["Read John 1:43-51 aloud together.", "Each person writes: What desires seem deepest in me? What gifts do I have? What do I fear? What is a next faithful step?", "In pairs, share one answer, and listen without advising.", "Choose one concrete step: talk to a priest, volunteer, make a retreat."],
    close="Pray the Suscipe or: “Lord, show me your will and give me the courage to follow.” Sign of the Cross. Schedule a follow-up conversation with a mentor.")),

'YA-7-2': dict(
  saint="Blessed Karl of Austria and Zita: an emperor and empress who prayed together, raised eight children and lost everything.",
  tryit=["Write the vows in your own words.", "List three things that make a marriage last.", "Write a prayer for your future marriage, or for a married couple you know."],
  saintStory="Karl of Habsburg was born in 1887 and became emperor of Austria-Hungary in 1916, in the middle of World War I, at twenty-nine. He had married Princess Zita of Bourbon-Parma in 1911. They had a deep faith, prayed together, and tried to live their marriage as a path of holiness. Their eight children were born between 1913 and 1922.\nAs emperor, Karl worked for peace. He tried secretly to negotiate with the Allies. He banned the use of poison gas in his army and tried to improve social conditions. After Austria-Hungary collapsed in 1918, the family was exiled. In 1921 he tried twice to regain the throne of Hungary and failed. The family was sent to the island of Madeira, in poverty, in a damp house. Karl caught pneumonia and died on April 1, 1922, at thirty-four. His last words to Zita: “I love you endlessly.”\nZita raised their children in exile, and lived to be ninety-six, dying in 1989. Karl was beatified in 2004; Zita’s cause is open. They show how marriage can be a vocation of holiness through great trials.",
  script=dict(
    open="Show a photo of a wedding from decades ago and one from now. Ask, “What has changed in how we imagine marriage? What has stayed the same?” Collect the two lists and compare.",
    teach=["Say: “The Church teaches that marriage is a covenant between a baptized man and woman, a lifelong partnership ordered to the good of the spouses and the procreation and education of children, raised by Christ to the dignity of a sacrament (CCC 1601). Its properties: unity (one man and one woman), indissolubility (permanent), fidelity, openness to life. The spouses are the ministers of the sacrament to each other. They exchange vows: ‘I take you to be my wife/husband, to have and to hold from this day forward, for better, for worse, for richer, for poorer, in sickness and in health, until death do us part.’ Paul compares marriage to Christ’s love for the Church (Ephesians 5:25-33). The sacrament gives grace for living this love: forgiveness, patience, sacrifice, fidelity.”", "Say: “What makes it work? Grace and virtue: communication, forgiveness, generosity, humility, patience, humor. Shared faith and prayer. Commitment: marriage is a decision of the will, not only feeling. Openness to children: the gift of fertility and the education of children. Sacrifice: marriage is a school of self-giving. Community: friends, family and parish. Marriage preparation: the Church requires it (often six months); it covers communication, finances, family planning, faith. NFP. Challenges: money, in-laws, infertility, illness, infidelity, boredom, stress. Resources: counselors, retreats (such as Marriage Encounter and Retrouvaille), priests. Marriage involves suffering as well as joy: Karl and Zita’s story shows that. Success is not a matter of avoiding hard times but going through them together with God.”", "Say: “The family. The Church calls the family the ‘domestic church.’ Parents are the first teachers of the faith. Family life includes prayer together, meals, Sunday Mass, forgiveness, service. Couples without children: infertility is a cross; adoption, spiritual fruitfulness. Single parents, blended families, divorced and remarried: the Church ministers with care. If you hope to marry: grow in virtue now; choose wisely; live chastely; learn to listen; pray for your spouse; take preparation seriously. If you’re already married: invest in the relationship, pray together, forgive often. And if marriage isn’t your call, support married friends. Vocations need each other. Pope Francis in Amoris Laetitia describes love in family life through 1 Corinthians 13: patient, kind, not jealous, not rude, not irritable, forgives, hopes. ‘Love is the vocation of every human being.’”"],
    ask=["What are the properties of marriage? (Unity and indissolubility; fidelity and openness to life.)", "Who are the ministers of the sacrament? (The spouses.)", "What are some things that help a marriage last? (Grace, forgiveness, prayer, communication, commitment, community.)"],
    practice=["Write the vows in your own words as a promise you would be ready to keep.", "List three habits that sustain a marriage, and a couple you admire who has them.", "Read 1 Corinthians 13:4-7, replacing ‘love’ with your name; mark where you grow.", "Write a prayer for your future marriage or for a couple you know."],
    close="Pray for married couples and for those preparing for marriage. Sign of the Cross. Encourage the group to interview a couple married 20 or more years.")),

'YA-7-3': dict(
  saint="Saint André Bessette: a frail doorkeeper who welcomed millions to the Oratory of St. Joseph.",
  tryit=["Write one question for a priest, a religious and a single adult.", "Interview one of them.", "Write what surprised you."],
  saintStory="Alfred Bessette was born in 1845 near Montreal, the eighth of twelve children. His father died when he was nine, and his mother when he was twelve. He was frail, and had poor health his whole life. He was uneducated, worked as a farmhand, a shoemaker and a baker, and in the factories of New England. He prayed often, especially to St. Joseph.\nIn 1870, at twenty-five, he asked the Holy Cross Fathers at Notre Dame College in Montreal to accept him. The pastor wrote a note to the superior: “I am sending you a saint.” He was given the name Brother André and made doorkeeper of the college, a job he held for forty years. He welcomed visitors, ran errands, cleaned and cared for the sick. He prayed for them, and used oil from the lamp before St. Joseph’s statue to anoint them. Many reported healings.\nCrowds came. He dreamed of building a chapel for St. Joseph, and began with a small chapel on Mount Royal in 1904, which grew into the vast Oratory. He died in 1937 at ninety-one, and a million people filed past his coffin. He was canonized in 2010. He lived the religious life in simplicity.",
  script=dict(
    open="Ask: “When you hear ‘priest,’ ‘nun’ or ‘single’, what images come to mind? What are your impressions or questions?” Write them down without judging. Say, “Today we hear the Church’s account of these three paths, and give you the chance to talk with real people.”",
    teach=["Say: “Holy Orders: men called by Christ and the Church to serve as bishops, priests and deacons. Priests celebrate the Mass and sacraments, preach, guide a parish. In the Latin Church they are normally celibate, giving their whole heart to God and his people; in Eastern Catholic Churches, married men may be ordained. Diocesan priests serve in a diocese under the bishop; religious priests belong to orders (Jesuits, Dominicans, Franciscans) with specific charisms. Deacons: transitional (on the way to priesthood) or permanent (often married). What does the priestly vocation feel like? A call to service, often confirmed by others; years of formation (usually 6-8 years); a life of prayer, availability, sacrifice, and joy. Pray for vocations: ‘The harvest is plentiful but the laborers are few.’ (Matthew 9:37)”", "Say: “Consecrated life: men and women who profess the evangelical counsels of poverty, chastity and obedience, in community. Forms: monks and nuns (contemplative, enclosed), friars and sisters (active, in apostolic work), members of secular institutes (living in the world), consecrated virgins, hermits, and new communities. Their life is a sign of the Kingdom: they live now what all will live in heaven. Contemplatives pray for the world; active orders teach, heal, serve. Brother André’s life: a layman in religious life, in service. Many orders welcome ‘come and see’ weekends and discernment. Religious life is not escape but total gift. The Church teaches it’s a gift to the whole Church (Vita Consecrata). Questions to ask: Am I drawn to a life centered on prayer and community? To a particular charism? Do I feel peace when I imagine it?”", "Say: “Single life: people who remain unmarried, for a variety of reasons. Some choose it for the Kingdom, as lay missionaries, teachers, doctors, giving their time to the Church and the poor, sometimes making a private promise or vow. Some find themselves single without choosing it, and the Church recognizes this as a real cross and as a real vocation to live love in a different form. Single people are not second-class. The Catechism says everyone is called to chastity. Single life offers freedom for service, friendship and hospitality, but it requires community and intentional effort against loneliness. Jesus himself and Paul were single. John Paul II spoke of the ‘vocation to love,’ which includes all. It is important to discern with a spiritual director, to visit communities, and to be honest about your desires. Talk to people in each vocation.”"],
    ask=["What are the three degrees of Holy Orders? (Bishop, priest, deacon.)", "What are the evangelical counsels? (Poverty, chastity and obedience.)", "Is single life a vocation? (Yes, for the Kingdom or by circumstance; all are called to love.)"],
    practice=["Write three questions for a priest, a religious sister or brother, and a single adult.", "If possible, invite a panel or interview people in these vocations.", "Afterward, write what surprised you and what attracted or frightened you.", "Pray for each vocation, and contact a vocation director if you want to learn more."],
    close="Pray: “Lord of the harvest, send laborers into your harvest.” Sign of the Cross. Offer contact information for your diocesan vocation office.")),

'YA-8-1': dict(
  saint="Homobonus of Cremona: a merchant whose name means “good man,” and whose shop was his chapel.",
  tryit=["List your gifts and the world’s needs.", "Note where they overlap.", "Write a prayer for your work."],
  saintStory="Homobonus, or Omobono, was born in Cremona, Italy, in the twelfth century, the son of a merchant. He learned the trade of a tailor and cloth merchant. He married and became known as an honest businessman. He gave generously to the poor, and was known for his fair dealing and for settling disputes.\nHe went to Mass every morning. Once, according to a story, a man jeered that he gave away so much he would be poor; Homobonus replied that those who give to the poor never lack. People said that his supplies of cloth and wine were mysteriously replenished.\nOn November 13, 1197, while at Mass in the church of St. Giles, he lay prostrate in prayer, with his arms outstretched in the form of a cross. When the Gloria ended, he did not get up. He had died. The people of Cremona recognized him as a saint, and Pope Innocent III canonized him in 1199, within two years. He is the patron of tailors, shoemakers and business people. A merchant, not a monk, was recognized as a saint.",
  script=dict(
    open="Display a list of ten common first jobs and ask the group to rate each for ‘meaningful’ and ‘paid well.’ Notice the mismatch. Say, “Calling is a third question. What is it?”",
    teach=["Say: “Work is part of God’s plan from the beginning. Genesis 2:15: God put the man in the garden ‘to till it and keep it.’ After the fall, work became toilsome. Jesus the carpenter sanctified human work. Paul: ‘Whatever your task, work heartily, as serving the Lord and not men.’ (Colossians 3:23) The Church teaches the dignity of work (John Paul II, Laborem Exercens, 1981): work is for the person, not the person for work; it expresses our nature as creatures made in God’s image, who create; it supports ourselves and our families; it contributes to the common good. Work that isn’t paid also has dignity: caring for children, volunteering, studying. Unemployment, exploitation and overwork are injustices.”", "Say: “Calling and vocation. ‘Vocation’ includes work, but isn’t identical. A job can be a way of serving. Choosing a career: consider your gifts (what you’re good at), your passions, the needs of the world, and your commitments (family, faith). Questions: Will this let me serve others? Will it let me live my values? Will it allow time for family, prayer and Sunday? Can I do it with integrity? Parable of the talents (Matthew 25): use what you’re given. Not everyone will love their job; some work is hard or dull; it can be offered to God. Josemaría Escrivá taught that ordinary work can become a path to holiness: ‘sanctifying work, sanctifying oneself in work, sanctifying others through work.’ Saints like Homobonus show that holiness doesn’t require leaving the world.”", "Say: “Practical: be excellent; be honest (no cheating, corner-cutting, stealing time); treat colleagues with respect; be a person of integrity; avoid gossip; refuse unethical demands; seek fair pay and treat others fairly; keep Sunday; be generous. Be mindful of the temptation to make work an idol, with career above family and faith, or to use people as instruments. The Church warns against workaholism and also laziness. Pray at work: offer the day in the morning; brief prayers; the Angelus at noon. Find a community of Catholic professionals. If you face a conflict between conscience and work, seek counsel. If you lack a job, the Church supports you; the parish can help. And be grateful. Work is a way of loving God and neighbor.”"],
    ask=["What does Laborem Exercens teach? (Work is for the person; it has dignity.)", "What questions help discern a career? (Gifts, passions, the world’s needs, commitments, integrity.)", "How can work become holy? (Offer it to God, do it well, honestly and with love.)"],
    practice=["Make a chart: My gifts / What I love / What the world needs. Look for overlaps.", "Write three ways to bring integrity and charity to your current work or studies.", "Write a morning offering for your work.", "Share one commitment with a partner."],
    close="Pray the Morning Offering or the prayer of Josemaría: “Lord, may all my work be for your glory.” Sign of the Cross. Encourage the group to find a Catholic professional network.")),

'YA-8-2': dict(
  saint="John the Almsgiver: a patriarch who called the poor “my masters” and spent the Church’s wealth on them.",
  tryit=["Track your spending for a week.", "Mark what reflects your values.", "Set a giving goal."],
  saintStory="John was born around 560 in Cyprus, to a wealthy family. He married and had children, but his wife and children died, and he gave himself to charity. In 606, he was named patriarch of Alexandria, the second most important see in the Eastern Church. He was reluctant, but he accepted.\nHe began by ordering an inventory of the poor of the city: he found about 7,500 people who needed help. He called them “my masters and helpers,” because they helped him gain heaven. He gave away the Church’s revenues, and also the gifts that wealthy people gave him. Once a rich man offered him a gift, and John said that if he wanted to please him, he should give it to the poor. He opened hospitals and guesthouses and offered food to refugees when the Persians invaded.\nWhen the Persians attacked Egypt, he fled to Cyprus, where he died in 619. At the end he is said to have thanked God that he had almost nothing to leave behind.",
  script=dict(
    open="Pass out a blank sheet and ask each person to write the three largest categories of their spending last month, from memory. Compare with a partner. Say, “Memory shows what we notice. The bank statement shows what we value.”",
    teach=["Say: “Jesus talked about money more than almost any other subject apart from the Kingdom. ‘You cannot serve God and mammon.’ (Matthew 6:24) ‘Where your treasure is, there will your heart be also.’ (Matthew 6:21) The rich young man went away sad because he had great possessions (Mark 10:17-22). Zacchaeus gave half his goods to the poor. But Jesus did not condemn wealth as such; he warned about its dangers: it can choke the word (Mark 4:19), foster anxiety, and harden the heart. Paul: ‘The love of money is the root of all evils.’ (1 Timothy 6:10) The Church teaches the right of private property but also the universal destination of goods: God made the earth for all (CCC 2402-2406).”", "Say: “Generosity. Almsgiving is a duty of justice and charity (CCC 2447). ‘Give to everyone who begs from you.’ (Luke 6:30) Tobit 4:7-11: ‘Give alms from your possessions.’ The Church’s tradition of the tithe (10 percent) is a guide, not a legal requirement. St. John Chrysostom: ‘Not to enable the poor to share in our goods is to steal from them. The goods we possess are not ours, but theirs.’ How to give: first to the Church and parish; then to the poor, with attention to effectiveness; also give time and skills. Give cheerfully, regularly, proportionally, and sacrificially (2 Corinthians 9:7). Jesus praised the widow’s mite (Mark 12:41-44). Give quietly (Matthew 6:3-4). Practical: set up automatic giving; choose causes; review your budget through prayer.”", "Say: “Justice. Charity gives to the person in need; justice asks why they are in need. The Church teaches about just wages, fair prices, debt, usury, consumerism and the global economy. Be a conscientious consumer: ask where products come from; avoid waste; resist advertising’s message that you need more. Simplicity of life. Personal finances: avoid debt, save, plan, do not gamble away money. Student debt and the cost of living are real; the Church recognizes this. Vocational decisions: don’t let money be the only factor. For work: seek just pay; for employers: pay just wages. John the Almsgiver’s approach: he saw the poor as Christ himself (Matthew 25:40). The question to ask each month: Where did my money go? Does it reflect what I say I value? Giving frees us from greed.”"],
    ask=["What did Jesus say about serving God and mammon? (We cannot serve both.)", "What is the universal destination of goods? (God made the earth for everyone.)", "How should we give? (Regularly, proportionally, cheerfully, sacrificially, often quietly.)"],
    practice=["Review last month’s spending by category. Mark in green what reflects your values and in red what doesn’t.", "Set a giving goal, as a percentage or an amount, and choose where it goes.", "Plan one act of time-giving with the poor this month.", "Share one change you will make with a partner."],
    close="Pray: “Lord, free me from the love of money and make me generous.” Sign of the Cross. Encourage the group to make a monthly giving plan.")),

'YA-8-3': dict(
  saint="Louise de Marillac: a widow and mother who organized the first sisters to serve the poor in the streets.",
  tryit=["Apply see-judge-act to a local problem.", "Name the Catholic social principles involved.", "Choose one action."],
  saintStory="Louise de Marillac was born in Paris in 1591, probably the daughter of a nobleman and a servant. She was never legitimized, which marked her. She was educated by Dominican nuns. She wanted to be a nun, but was told her health was too fragile. In 1613 she married Antoine Le Gras, a royal secretary, and had a son, Michel. She cared for her husband through a long illness before he died in 1625.\nIn 1624, she met Vincent de Paul, who became her spiritual director. He was organizing charitable groups called the Confraternities of Charity. He saw that Louise had a gift for organization. She traveled around France, setting up groups and training volunteers. In 1633, she gathered a few young peasant women in her home to train them in nursing and service of the poor. This was the beginning of the Daughters of Charity, who did not live in an enclosed convent but went out into the streets to serve the sick, the poor, orphans and prisoners. Vincent told them their convent was the home of the sick, their cell a rented room, their chapel the parish church, their cloister the streets of the city.\nLouise led the Daughters until her death in 1660. She was canonized in 1934, and named patron of social workers.",
  script=dict(
    open="Ask: “What is one problem in your community that you wish someone would fix? Who is hurt by it? What do you think causes it?” Write several on a board. Say, “Catholic social teaching gives tools to think and act.”",
    teach=["Say: “Catholic social teaching is the Church’s body of teaching about society, from Rerum Novarum (1891) to Laudato Si’ (2015). Its seven themes (US bishops): life and dignity of the human person; call to family, community and participation; rights and responsibilities; option for the poor and vulnerable; the dignity of work and the rights of workers; solidarity; care for God’s creation. Key principles: human dignity (every person is made in God’s image); the common good (the conditions allowing all to flourish); subsidiarity (decisions at the closest competent level); solidarity (we are one human family); the universal destination of goods. It is sometimes called the Church’s best-kept secret, because it is little known.”", "Say: “A method: See, Judge, Act. Cardinal Joseph Cardijn, who founded the Young Christian Workers, developed it for lay people. See: look honestly at the facts. Who is affected? What are the causes? Judge: evaluate in light of the Gospel and Church teaching. What does dignity ask? What does justice require? Act: decide on specific steps. St. John XXIII’s Mater et Magistra endorsed the method. Examples. Homelessness: see (numbers, causes), judge (dignity, option for the poor), act (volunteer, advocate for housing, give). Immigration: see (stories, laws), judge (the Holy Family were refugees; nations may regulate borders but must welcome as they can), act (welcome, support legal aid, pray). Poverty, racism, human trafficking, the environment, abortion, war.”", "Say: “Charity and justice. Charity meets immediate needs: soup kitchen. Justice addresses causes: policies, structures. Both are required. Louise’s approach: she combined direct service with organization. The Church warns against a purely political or purely private faith. Catholic social teaching doesn’t endorse a party; it challenges every party. How to engage: pray; learn (read the Compendium of the Social Doctrine, the US bishops’ Faithful Citizenship); join (St. Vincent de Paul Society, Catholic Charities, Catholic Relief Services, Catholic Worker, JustFaith groups); vote; volunteer; advocate by writing officials; live simply. Avoid despair: change is slow. Mother Teresa said, ‘Not all of us can do great things. But we can do small things with great love.’ Choose one issue and stay with it.”"],
    ask=["Name three principles of Catholic social teaching. (Dignity, common good, solidarity, subsidiarity, option for the poor.)", "What are the steps of See-Judge-Act? (Observe the facts; evaluate by the Gospel; decide on action.)", "What is the difference between charity and justice? (Charity meets needs; justice addresses causes.)"],
    practice=["Choose a real issue in your community. Use See-Judge-Act on a sheet: facts, principles, action.", "Name the Catholic social principles involved.", "Choose one action you can do in the next month, and a person who will check in.", "Find an organization in your area (St. Vincent de Paul, Catholic Charities) and contact them."],
    close="Pray: “Lord, make us instruments of your peace and justice.” Sign of the Cross. Encourage the group to join a local service group.")),
}
